The original scans `mesgs[]` linearly on every call. This version builds a 65536-entry index table indexed by message number, once, on the first call. After that, each lookup is one array read.

- **Same results:** the table is filled from the end of the array toward the start, so when two entries share a `num`, the earliest one wins, as in the original loop. Numbers with no entry still return `NULL`.
- **Safe to build once:** the table depends only on the constant `mesgs` array, not on any mutable state. C++11 and later guarantee that function-local static initialization runs exactly once, even with concurrent callers.
- **Fallback:** if `MESGS` were ever too large to index with 16 bits, the code falls back to the original linear scan.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    static FIT_UINT16 index[65536];
    static const bool initialized = []() -> bool
    {
        for (int k = 0; k < 65536; k++)
            index[k] = 0xFFFF;
        for (int i = MESGS - 1; i >= 0; i--)
            index[mesgs[i].num] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
        return true;
    }();
    (void)initialized;

    if (MESGS < 0xFFFF)
    {
        const FIT_UINT16 i = index[num];
        if (i == 0xFFFF)
            return NULL;
        return &mesgs[i];
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
    {
        if (mesgs[i].num == num)
            return &mesgs[i];
    }
    return NULL;
}
```